# TEST-03 — Idempotent Consumer

## Objective

Verify that receiving the same logical event twice does not execute the business operation twice.

The important identifier here is:

```text
eventId
```

not the Kafka key / `aggregateId`.

---

## Step 1 — Process a Normal Event

Create an order normally.

Example:

```text
aggregateId =
11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555
```

The Outbox created an event with:

```text
eventId =
78d61358-7364-4d30-87f2-821ef7cd8106
```

The consumer processed it successfully.

The `eventId` is now stored in:

```text
processed_messages
```

---

## Step 2 — Send the Same `eventId` Again

Manually publish another Kafka record using exactly the same `eventId`:

```bash
printf 'eventId:78d61358-7364-4d30-87f2-821ef7cd8106\t11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555\t{"test":"duplicate-event"}\n' | \
docker exec -i kafka-1 /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:29092 \
  --topic order-events-secure \
  --property parse.headers=true \
  --property parse.key=true
```

---

## Expected Result

The second delivery must not execute the business logic.

Expected log:

```text
Duplicate order event ignored
[eventId=78d61358-7364-4d30-87f2-821ef7cd8106]
```

---

## Internal Flow

First delivery:

```text
eventId
↓
INSERT processed_messages
↓
success
↓
business logic executed
↓
COMMIT
```

Second delivery:

```text
same eventId
↓
INSERT processed_messages
↓
primary-key / unique constraint violation
↓
duplicate detected
↓
business logic NOT executed
```

---

## Important

A duplicate is considered successfully handled.

Therefore it is:

```text
NOT retried
NOT sent to the DLT
NOT processed again
```

---

## What This Proves

Kafka may deliver the same logical event more than once.

The application guarantees:

```text
at-least-once delivery
+
idempotent consumer
=
single business effect
```

---

# TEST-04 — Rollback of Consumer Idempotency

## Objective

Verify that an event is not permanently marked as processed when its business processing fails.

---

## Transaction Under Test

```java
@Transactional
public void process(
        String messageId,
        String payload,
        Runnable businessLogic
) {

    repository.insertIdempotent(
            messageId,
            LocalDateTime.now()
    );

    businessLogic.run();
}
```

Both operations belong to the same database transaction:

```text
INSERT processed_messages
+
business processing
```

---

## Temporary Test Preparation

Temporarily throw an exception inside the business processing:

```java
idempotentProcessor.process(eventId, payload, () -> {

    throw new RuntimeException(
            "Intentional rollback test"
    );
});
```

---

## Expected Behavior

```text
BEGIN TRANSACTION
↓
INSERT eventId
↓
business processing
↓
RuntimeException
↓
ROLLBACK
```

The `processed_messages` insertion must be rolled back too.

---

## Observed Result

The same event:

```text
eventId =
975c60cc-061d-44ac-985d-7af6faa7d7b1
```

was accepted during every Kafka retry.

Four business-processing attempts were observed.

---

## Why This Proves the Rollback

If the first `processed_messages` insert had been committed:

```text
Attempt 1
→ INSERT eventId
→ business failure
→ eventId remains in DB
```

then retry 1 would have immediately produced:

```text
Duplicate event
```

Instead, every retry entered the business processing again.

Therefore:

```text
business failure
→ transaction rollback
→ eventId insert rollback
→ Kafka retry allowed
```

---

## Cleanup

Remove the temporary exception after the test.

