# TEST-05 — Transactional Outbox During Kafka Failure

## Objective

Verify that a committed business operation does not lose its domain event when Kafka is unavailable.

This is the main property of the Transactional Outbox Pattern.

---

## Initial State

The application writes:

```text
Order
+
OutboxEvent
```

inside the business database transaction.

Kafka publication happens later through:

```text
OutboxKafkaRelay
```

---

## Step 1 — Stop Kafka

```bash
docker stop kafka-1 kafka-2 kafka-3
```

---

## Step 2 — Create an Order

Use a new UUID:

```bash
curl -X POST \
  http://localhost:8080/api/v1/orders/77777777-7777-7777-7777-777777777777
```

---

## Expected Database State

Even though Kafka is unavailable:

```text
Order             → stored
OutboxEvent       → stored
processed         → false
Kafka publication → failed
```

The business request does not depend on immediate Kafka availability.

---

## Relay Behavior

The Relay runs periodically:

```java
@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
```

Every five seconds:

```text
find processed=false
↓
attempt Kafka publication
↓
Kafka unavailable
↓
publication fails
↓
processed remains false
```

---

## Step 3 — Restart Kafka

```bash
docker start kafka-1 kafka-2 kafka-3
```

Do **not** repeat the HTTP POST.

---

## Expected Result

Once Kafka is available again:

```text
Relay finds existing processed=false event
↓
publication succeeds
↓
Kafka ACK
↓
processed=true
↓
consumer receives event
```

---

## What This Proves

Without an Outbox:

```text
Order committed
↓
Kafka unavailable
↓
event potentially lost
```

With the Outbox:

```text
Order committed
+
event intent persisted
↓
Kafka unavailable
↓
event remains pending
↓
Kafka returns
↓
event published automatically
```

This validates the core purpose of the **Transactional Outbox Pattern**.

