# TEST-06 — One Kafka Broker Unavailable

## Objective

Verify that Kafka can still accept publications after losing one broker.

Configuration:

```text
Replication Factor = 3
min.insync.replicas = 2
acks = all
```

---

## Step 1 — Stop One Broker

```bash
docker stop kafka-3
```

---

## Step 2 — Inspect the Topic

```bash
docker exec kafka-1 /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:29092 \
  --describe \
  --topic order-events-secure
```

Observed:

```text
Partition 0 → Isr: 1,2
Partition 1 → Isr: 1,2
Partition 2 → Isr: 1,2
```

---

## Step 3 — Produce a New Event

Create another Order using a unique UUID.

Example:

```text
66666666-6666-6666-6666-666666666669
```

---

## Expected Result

Publication still succeeds because:

```text
ISR = 2
minISR = 2
```

Therefore:

```text
2 ISR >= minISR
→ Kafka accepts the write
```

---

## What This Proves

The cluster tolerates one broker failure without losing write availability.

```text
3 brokers
↓
1 broker fails
↓
2 synchronized replicas remain
↓
publication still possible
```

---

# TEST-07 — Insufficient ISR Prevents Publication

## Objective

Verify that Kafka refuses a successful write when the configured durability level can no longer be satisfied.

Configuration:

```text
acks = all
min.insync.replicas = 2
```

---

## Initial State

Keep `kafka-3` stopped.

Then reduce the number of available nodes further.

Example:

```bash
docker stop kafka-2
```

Only one broker remains.

---

## Expected Result

The producer attempts publication, but Kafka cannot satisfy:

```text
min.insync.replicas = 2
```

Therefore the publication cannot be confirmed.

---

## Expected Outbox State

The Order remains committed.

The corresponding Outbox event remains:

```text
processed=false
```

because the code only executes:

```java
entity.setProcessed(true);
```

after:

```java
kafkaTemplate.send(record).get();
```

has succeeded.

---

## Expected Flow

```text
Order stored
↓
Outbox event stored
↓
Kafka send attempted
↓
not enough available/synchronized replicas
↓
send fails
↓
processed remains false
```

When the cluster becomes healthy again:

```text
Relay retries
↓
send succeeds
↓
processed=true
```

---

## What This Proves

Kafka prefers temporarily refusing writes rather than acknowledging writes below the configured durability requirement.

The Outbox preserves the event until publication becomes possible again.

---

## Important KRaft Note

The three containers in this project are combined:

```text
broker + KRaft controller
```

With only one of three nodes alive, the KRaft controller quorum also loses its majority.

Therefore this particular failure test demonstrates the expected cluster unavailability, but it does not isolate `min.insync.replicas` from the controller-quorum constraint.

