# TEST-08 — Replica Catch-Up After Broker Restart

## Objective

Verify that a broker which was offline retrieves the records produced while it was unavailable.

---

## Initial State

During TEST-06:

```text
kafka-3 = stopped
```

A record was produced while only brokers 1 and 2 were in the ISR.

Example:

```text
aggregateId =
66666666-6666-6666-6666-666666666669
```

---

## Step 1 — Check ISR Before Restart

```bash
docker exec kafka-1 /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:29092 \
  --describe \
  --topic order-events-secure
```

Before `kafka-3` returned:

```text
Replicas: 3,1,2
Isr:      1,2
```

This means:

```text
broker 3 = assigned replica
broker 3 = not synchronized
```

---

## Step 2 — Restart Broker 3

```bash
docker start kafka-3
```

---

## Step 3 — Inspect the Topic Again

```bash
docker exec kafka-1 /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:29092 \
  --describe \
  --topic order-events-secure
```

Observed after recovery:

```text
Partition 0
Leader: 2
Replicas: 2,3,1
Isr: 1,2,3

Partition 1
Leader: 3
Replicas: 3,1,2
Isr: 1,2,3

Partition 2
Leader: 1
Replicas: 1,2,3
Isr: 1,2,3
```

---

## Expected Interpretation

```text
kafka-3 restarts
↓
detects missing records
↓
fetches missing partition data
↓
catches up
↓
returns to ISR
```

---

## Important

Replica catch-up is **not consumer processing**.

Kafka internally copying the record:

```text
broker 1 → broker 3
```

does not cause the Spring consumer to execute the business logic again.

---

## What This Proves

Kafka can recover replica consistency after temporary broker failure.

